{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal acknowledges that adversarial query sequences may defeat memoization and break the polynomial-cost bound. The problem requires preserving polynomial cost as a guarantee, not merely avoiding additional stored state. By replacing eager materialization with demand-driven computation, a query for every derived artifact on every node at each fixed-point iteration would force recomputation proportional to the full extension size per query, yielding super-polynomial total cost (e.g., querying all DefenseWitness predicates across all nodes defeats memoization deterministically and violates the polynomial-cost guarantee the problem demands be preserved).","target":"d9eec18ececba7fce3e377dd1f8f4f5c9c1764f47eae0d77dc9769000e037e54"},{"attack":true,"case":"The suspension-stability axiom directly conflicts with reinstatement as a derived property, which the problem requires be preserved. Consider a graph where node x is undecided because its sole defense path is length k+1. The axiom forbids any bounded-depth support chain addition from flipping x to accepted unless x already had a defense path of length ≤ k. But reinstatement legitimately allows a previously undecided node to become accepted after arguments are added that defeat its attackers. The axiom blocks exactly those legitimate reinstatement scenarios where the new support chain is itself the mechanism by which x gains a valid defense, making reinstatement no longer derivable for that class of graphs.","target":"e2a71196cb02ba0a2d95014152e9a568febed0ed8d8172e391d7fcee9a26c70d"}]}